 Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+
+# Spreadsheet completion protocol
+
+Write requirements as **directional ledger entries**, not as "adjustment" labels.
+A label such as "Non-recurring expense", "Gain", "Credit", "Charge", "Write-down"
+or "Repayment" is ambiguous about sign and direction. Before writing a formula,
+decide for EACH item whether normalizing means:
+
+- ADDING a benefit back (remove an expense from the expense base), or
+- SUBTRACTING a benefit removed (take a gain out of the income base).
+
+Resolve the direction from how the reported figures reconcile, never from the
+label alone. Sanity-check: reported pre-tax results should already be internally
+consistent with the reported expense and gain lines. If a formula moves a
+line-item in a direction that makes the reported base inconsistent, the
+direction is wrong.
+
+## Rate scoping
+
+When a workbook gives a rate per adjustment line, apply that rate ONLY to that
+line's own amount, at that line's own magnitude, per period. Never apply an
+adjustment rate to the total base, and never let one adjustment's rate cascade
+onto another adjustment. Tax effects of each adjustment are additive to the
+reported tax figure:
+
+  normalized_tax = reported_tax +/- (line_amount * line_rate) for each line
+
+Sign follows the same direction test as the base item: removing an expense
+restores the tax it sheltered; removing a gain removes the tax it attracted.
+
+## Derived rows
+
+Compute derived rows (net profit, margins, subtotals, balances) by formula from
+the rows above them so the arithmetic stays transparent and auditable. Margins
+divide by the same-period revenue; never divide by a stale or zero denominator.
+Keep every row in a column aligned to the same period.
+
+## Formula hygiene
+
+Use real Excel formulas referencing cells (never hard-coded results or pasted
+engine-computed numbers), because an independent engine recalculates them.
+Reference input cells relatively so formulas can copy across the period columns.
+Use absolute references only for true constants such as opening balances and
+fixed rates.
+
+## Verification before delivery
+
+Recalculate with LibreOffice headless and read the computed values back, e.g.
+  soffice --headless --convert-to xlsx --outdir /tmp/chk <file>
+then load the converted file with openpyxl (data_only=True). Confirm every
+required cell is non-blank, has no #REF!/#DIV/0!/#VALUE! error, and lands in a
+plausible range. Confirm the existing inputs, labels and number formats survived.
+
+## Deliverable discipline
+
+Write exactly the requested output path (e.g. /workspace/result.xlsx) from a
+copy of the source workbook so original inputs remain intact. Leave cells
+declared intentionally blank untouched. Do not add, delete or rename rows,
+columns, sheets or labels. Do not fill totals the task says to leave empty.
+Read every cell comment before modeling; comments often carry the governing
+convention (rate scope, sign, timing) that the labels omit.
